OHIRA-0006

Knowledge Graph & Data Model

Project: Ohira

Document ID: OHIRA-0006

Version: 1.0

Status: Founder's Draft


---

Purpose

The Knowledge Graph is Ohira's core intelligence model.

Its purpose is to transform software projects from collections of files into structured, interconnected knowledge.

Rather than asking:

> "Which files exist?"



Ohira answers:

What features exist?

Which APIs belong to a module?

Which database models support a feature?

Which routes require authentication?

Which services communicate together?

What business capability does this module implement?



---

Design Philosophy

Traditional tools understand files.

Ohira understands software systems.

Everything becomes a graph of entities connected by meaningful relationships.


---

Core Concepts

The Knowledge Graph is composed of:

Nodes (entities)

Relationships (edges)

Properties (metadata)

Context (analysis results)


Project
   │
   ├── Modules
   ├── APIs
   ├── Database
   ├── Infrastructure
   ├── Documentation
   └── Features


---

Primary Node Types

Project

Represents the software project.

Example:

{
  "id": "project",
  "name": "Smart Property",
  "type": "Project"
}


---

Module

Logical business capabilities.

Examples:

Authentication

Users

Properties

Bookings

Payments

Trust System

Vendors


A module may contain:

Routes

Services

Models

APIs

Events



---

Feature

Represents a user-facing capability.

Examples:

User Registration

Property Search

Rent Payment

Token Purchase

Estate Bill Payment



---

Route

Represents an application endpoint.

Example:

GET /api/users

POST /api/properties

PATCH /api/bookings/:id

Metadata:

HTTP Method

Authentication

Module

Version



---

Service

Contains business logic.

Examples:

PaymentService

PropertyService

TrustService

BookingService


---

Entity

Represents persistent business objects.

Examples:

User

Property

Booking

Payment

Token

Vendor

Usually mapped from Prisma models or ORM schemas.


---

Database

Represents the underlying datastore.

Metadata:

Provider

Version

ORM

Connection strategy



---

Plugin

Represents an Ohira plugin.

Examples:

Prisma Plugin

Docker Plugin

Git Plugin

Next Plugin

Plugins contribute knowledge to the graph.


---

Infrastructure

Represents runtime components.

Examples:

Docker

Redis

MySQL

Nginx

