---

Metadata

Every node includes metadata such as:

{
  "createdAt": "...",
  "updatedAt": "...",
  "plugin": "@ohira/plugin-prisma",
  "confidence": 0.99,
  "source": "schema.prisma"
}

This supports traceability and debugging.


---

AI Context Model

The graph can be summarized into AI-friendly context.

Example:

{
  "project": "Smart Property",
  "stack": {
    "framework": "Next.js",
    "runtime": "Node.js",
    "database": "MySQL",
    "orm": "Prisma",
    "authentication": "Clerk"
  },
  "modules": [
    "Users",
    "Properties",
    "Bookings",
    "Payments",
    "Trust"
  ]
}

This compact representation allows AI assistants to understand the project without scanning the entire repository.


---

Queries

The Knowledge Graph should support queries such as:

Which modules use Prisma?

Which routes require authentication?

Which services depend on Docker?

Which features are incomplete?

Which entities are unused?

Which APIs expose payment functionality?


Future versions may expose these through a graph query API.


---

Incremental Updates

The graph should not be rebuilt from scratch for every change.

Instead:

1. Detect file changes.


2. Re-analyze affected components.


3. Update only impacted nodes and relationships.


4. Preserve unchanged knowledge.



This enables fast analysis on large projects.


---

Storage

Initially, the graph can be stored as JSON files.

Future options include:

SQLite

PostgreSQL

Neo4j

Amazon Neptune

Other graph databases


The storage layer should remain abstract so implementations can evolve without changing the graph model.


---

Knowledge Levels

Ohira classifies information by confidence:

Observed – directly extracted from project files.

Derived – inferred from relationships.

Declared – provided by the developer through configuration.

Generated – produced by AI or higher-level analysis.


This helps users distinguish facts from inferences.


